    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "When you think about your interpretation of what happened with your father's death, is there any part of you that wonders whether you might be misinterpreting the evidence, or are you completely certain that someone is definitely hiding something?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S019",
      "patient_response": "Am I misinterpreting it? No, no, I'm completely certain. It's not a matter of interpretation, it's a matter of looking at the raw data. Like I said, I know everyone keeps telling me the hospital records and those donation emails all line up. My sister, the doctors, everyone just wants it to be this neat little package. But they aren't looking at the metadata. \n\nIt reminds me of when I was trying to buy a used car a few years ago. The dealership gave me this pristine Carfax report, right? Said it had only one owner, no accidents, regular maintenance. But I brought my own OBD-II scanner—you know, the thing you plug under the dashboard to read the engine codes? The salesman got so defensive when I pulled it out. And sure enough, the computer had been wiped recently, and the airbag deployment module had a mismatched serial number. I ended up buying a Honda Civic from a private seller instead. Great gas mileage, honestly, though the AC compressor went out last summer. \n\nBut that's exactly my point. People will hand you a piece of paper that says everything is fine, but when I compare the timestamps on those hospital files... and I look at the missing email attachments... and then there's the way certain community members just completely avoid my questions when I bring it up? It’s obvious to me. Someone is hiding what really happened around my father’s death. I'm not misinterpreting anything; I'm just the only one actually looking at the evidence.",
